1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Welcome back. A design can look clean on the whiteboard and still fail the requirements it was built for.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Solution architecture fit is the check that catches that. It asks a blunt question.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Does this proposed design actually satisfy every constraint and requirement that we stated?

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
This lesson covers reliability, failover, and how to judge fit without fooling yourself.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Let's be precise about what fit means, because it's stricter than it sounds.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Fit is a verdict, not a feeling. Either the design satisfies a given constraint, or it doesn't.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
So the method is simple. Before you evaluate anything, list every constraint the requirement stated.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Then check the design against each one in turn. And here's the part people resist.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
A single, unmet constraint means the design doesn't fit as specified.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
You either fix it, or you explicitly renegotiate the constraint and record the tradeoff.

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
What you never do is wave it through on elegance.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
If it misses the latency target or lands data in the wrong region, it doesn't fit as stated.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Fit is measured against the requirement, never against your own taste for a design.

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
That discipline is what keeps a good-looking answer from shipping broken.

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
A lot of fit failures hide in reliability, so let's name the three requirements there.

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The first is availability, the share of time the application has to be usable.

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
A number here forces you to plan for the moments your primary path is down, because it will be at some point.

18
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The second is failover, a defined path to a backup when the primary call fails.

19
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Without it, one outage takes the whole feature down with it.

20
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:49,000
The third is degradation, a reduced but still useful experience when full service isn't possible.

21
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Instead of throwing a hard error in the user's face, you give them something.

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
These three often go unstated in the requirement, which is exactly why they get missed.

23
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
If reliability matters, make them explicit and design for them.

24
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Failover isn't one thing, it's a small toolkit, and three building blocks cover most cases.

25
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
The first is a fallback model.

26
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
When your primary model is unavailable, you root to an alternate one, keeping the feature alive,

27
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
even if the capability drops a notch.

28
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
The second is retry with backoff.

29
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
You resend a failed call after a growing delay, and the next slide pins down exactly which failures

30
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
deserve that treatment.

31
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
The third is graceful degradation.

32
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Instead of failing outright, you return a cached answer, a simpler answer, or a partial one, so the

33
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
user still gets something useful.

34
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
You don't need all three everywhere.

35
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
You pick the blocks that match the reliability requirement you actually have to meet.

36
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Recovery only works when it matches the error, so you have to classify first.

37
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Errors fall into two buckets.

38
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Transient errors are timeouts, rate limits, server blips, and network hiccups.

39
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
They're expected to clear on their own, so the correct response is a retry with exponential backoff.

40
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Permanent errors are different.

41
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
A bad request, an invalid schema, an authentication failure.

42
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Retrying those changes, nothing, because retrying without changing anything can't fix them.

43
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
The request, or the credential, is what's wrong.

44
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
They need a fix, a fallback, or an escalation to a human.

45
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The classic mistake is applying retry to everything.

46
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Retrying a permanent error just wastes budget and delays the moment you realize something is genuinely broken.

47
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So, identify the type first, then choose the strategy.

48
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The strategy has to match the type, not the symptom you happen to see.

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Reliability really lives in what your application does on its worst day, so design that day on purpose.

50
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Decide the fallback model before an outage forces the decision under pressure, not during the incident.

51
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Give the client a degraded path instead of a blank screen.

52
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:13,000
A cached result, a shorter answer, a clear message with a retry, any of those beats a spinner that never resolves.

53
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Then map each recovery to the classification you just made so the right block from the toolkit fires

54
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,000
for the right failure.

55
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
The teams that get burned are the ones who only ever tested the happy path.

56
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
The bad day always arrives eventually.

57
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
If you've designed for it in advance, it's a minor event instead of an outage that surprises everyone.

58
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Here's how to actually run a fit check.

59
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Take each stated requirement and turn it into a question the design has to answer out loud.

60
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Latency budget? Count the sequential model calls in the design and see if they fit inside the number.

61
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Throughput target? Check it against the rate limits you'll be working under.

62
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Residency rule? Confirm where the calls physically run and whether that's allowed.

63
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Availability target? Point to the failover path or honestly admit there isn't one yet.

64
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,000
The value of phrasing them as questions is that it exposes the gaps.

65
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
A hand wave doesn't survive a direct question.

66
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
If any of these has no good answer, the design isn't finished, no matter how complete it looks on the diagram.

67
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
That's the whole discipline in one habit.

68
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Three fit failures show up again and again, so learn to spot them.

69
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
The first is over-provisioning.

70
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
You reach for real-time capacity when the work was actually deferrable,

71
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
so you pay premium latency and premium cost for speed nobody needed.

72
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
A nightly job doesn't need a streaming response.

73
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
The second is under-provisioning.

74
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:47,000
You ship with no failover and no headroom,

75
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
and the first rate limit spike or brief outage takes the entire feature down.

76
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
It worked in the demo and died under load.

77
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
The third is wrong placement.

78
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
A design is technically sound, but it ignores a residency or region constraint,

79
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
so it's legally or contractually unusable, no matter how well it runs.

80
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Notice these are all fit problems.

81
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
The code might be fine.

82
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
The match to the requirement is what broke.

83
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Let's turn all of this into one repeatable habit.

84
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Every time you evaluate a design, walk the same short checklist.

85
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
First, write down every constraint, both the functional behaviors

86
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
and the infrastructure requirements, latency, throughput, region, cost, availability.

87
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Second, trace the design against each constraint one at a time,

88
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
no skipping the inconvenient ones.

89
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Third, flag anything the design can't clearly satisfy.

90
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
And fourth, either fix it or consciously record the tradeoff

91
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
before you declare the design done.

92
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:54,000
The point of the checklist is that fit is easy to fake when you eyeball it

93
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
and hard to fake when you go item by item.

94
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
A design fits only when nothing on that list is left unanswered.

95
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Make that your finish line, and shaky architectures stop reaching production. Let's recap.

96
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Solution architecture fit means the design satisfies every stated constraint,

97
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
not just most of them.

98
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:20,000
Plan reliability deliberately with availability, failover, and degradation.

99
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Retry transient errors, but fix or fall back on permanent ones.

100
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Just remember, over-provisioning, under-provisioning, and wrong placement

101
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
are the misses to watch for.

102
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,000
That wraps up matching architecture to the requirement.

103
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,000
I'll see you in the next one.

